> **THC is the compound responsible for cannabis's psychoactive effects — but the full picture is more interesting than that.** This guide covers what THC is, how it works in the body, what to expect at different doses, and how consumption method changes everything.

---

## What is THC?

THC — tetrahydrocannabinol — is the primary psychoactive cannabinoid in the cannabis plant. It's the compound responsible for the sensation commonly called being "high": the euphoria, altered perception, relaxation, and increased appetite that cannabis is known for.

THC is one of over 100 cannabinoids identified in cannabis, but it's present in the highest concentrations in most commercial strains and has been the most studied. It's produced in the plant's trichomes — the tiny, crystal-like structures that coat the flowers and sugar leaves — alongside other cannabinoids, terpenes, and aromatic compounds.

Modern cannabis flower typically contains 15–30% THC by dry weight. Concentrates can reach 60–90%.

---

## THC vs. THCA: the raw vs. activated distinction

Here's something most people don't know: raw cannabis flower doesn't actually contain much THC. It contains **THCA** — tetrahydrocannabinolic acid — a non-intoxicating precursor that needs heat to convert into active THC.

This conversion process is called **decarboxylation**, and it happens automatically when you smoke or vape cannabis (the heat does it instantly). For edibles and infusions, you have to apply heat deliberately before cooking — a step called decarbing.

If you ate raw cannabis, the THCA wouldn't produce psychoactive effects. This is why decarboxylation is the essential first step in any edibles process. See our [complete decarboxylation guide](/edibles/decarb-activate-cannabis-edibles/) for how to do it properly.

---

## How THC works in the body

To understand THC, you need to know a little about the **endocannabinoid system (ECS)** — a biological regulatory network present in all mammals. The ECS uses its own naturally produced cannabinoids (endocannabinoids) to regulate mood, memory, appetite, sleep, pain, and immune function.

The two main endocannabinoids are **anandamide** (sometimes called the "bliss molecule") and **2-AG**. They bind to receptors throughout the brain and body — primarily **CB1 receptors** (concentrated in the brain and central nervous system) and **CB2 receptors** (concentrated in the immune system and peripheral tissues).

THC works because its molecular structure closely resembles anandamide. It binds to CB1 receptors in the brain, mimicking and amplifying the effects of the body's own endocannabinoids. This binding — particularly in areas of the brain involved in memory, pleasure, coordination, and time perception — produces THC's characteristic effects.

The reason cannabis feels different from your own endocannabinoid system at work is partly dose: THC floods receptors at levels far beyond what the body naturally produces, and it stays bound for longer.

---

## What THC actually feels like

Effects vary significantly by dose, consumption method, tolerance, and individual biology — but here's an honest account of what THC produces at different levels:

### At low doses (2.5–10mg or light smoking)
- Mild euphoria and mood lift
- Slightly heightened sensory perception (food tastes better, music sounds richer)
- Relaxation without sedation
- Mild increase in appetite
- Slight time distortion
- Increased sociability for some people

### At moderate doses (10–25mg or moderate smoking)
- More pronounced euphoria
- Clearer perceptual changes — colors may seem more vivid, thoughts more associative
- Stronger body relaxation
- More significant time distortion
- Increased appetite
- Some people experience mild anxiety or self-consciousness at this range

### At high doses (25mg+ edibles or heavy smoking)
- Intense euphoria or sedation depending on strain and individual
- Significant perceptual changes
- Strong body effect
- Possible anxiety, paranoia, or racing thoughts — more common at high doses, especially in inexperienced users
- Impaired short-term memory and coordination

The most important thing to know: **THC affects people very differently.** Two people taking the same dose can have completely different experiences based on their tolerance, genetics, what they've eaten, and their state of mind going in.

---

## How consumption method changes the THC experience

This is one of the most important and least understood aspects of THC. The same amount of THC behaves very differently depending on how you consume it.

| Method | Onset | Peak | Duration | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Smoking | 2–10 min | 20–30 min | 1–3 hrs | Fast, predictable, easier to control |
| Vaping | 5–15 min | 20–40 min | 1–3 hrs | Similar to smoking, less combustion |
| Edibles | 30–120 min | 2–3 hrs | 4–8 hrs | Liver converts THC to 11-hydroxy-THC — stronger, longer |
| Tincture (sublingual) | 15–45 min | 45–90 min | 2–4 hrs | Faster than edibles, more controlled |
| Tincture (swallowed) | 30–90 min | 1.5–3 hrs | 4–6 hrs | Similar to edibles |

### Why edibles feel different

When you eat cannabis, THC passes through the digestive system to the liver before entering the bloodstream. The liver converts THC into **11-hydroxy-THC** — a metabolite that crosses the blood-brain barrier more readily than THC itself. This is why edibles often feel more intense and body-heavy than smoking the same amount, and why effects last 4–8 hours rather than 1–3.

It's also why the delayed onset of edibles causes so many problems. People conclude "nothing's happening" and take more — only to have both doses arrive at once. **Always wait the full 2 hours before re-dosing.**

See our [Cannabis Edibles 101 guide](/edibles/cooking-with-cannabis-edibles-101/) for a deep dive on how edibles work.

---

## Dosing reference

| Experience level | Starting dose (edibles) | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Complete beginner | 2.5–5mg THC | Start here regardless of smoking experience |
| Some experience | 5–10mg THC | Standard dispensary edible dose |
| Regular consumer | 10–25mg THC | |
| High tolerance | 25mg+ THC | Not recommended without established tolerance |

For homemade edibles, use our [Potency Calculator](/edibles-calculator/) to estimate THC per serving. Home infusions vary in potency and the calculator helps you dose your batches accurately.

---

## How THC interacts with CBD

CBD — the other major cannabinoid — modifies the THC experience in meaningful ways. CBD appears to reduce some of THC's anxiety-inducing effects, take the edge off the high, and extend the duration slightly.

Products with balanced THC:CBD ratios are often described as more manageable and less likely to cause anxiety than high-THC products alone. This is part of what's called the **entourage effect** — the idea that cannabinoids work better together than in isolation.

For more on this, see our What Is CBD? and What Is the Entourage Effect? guides.

---

## What affects your THC experience

Beyond dose and consumption method, several factors shape how THC hits:

**Tolerance** — Regular users develop tolerance to THC through downregulation of CB1 receptors. The same dose produces less effect over time. Tolerance breaks (periods of abstinence) reset this.

**Body composition** — THC is fat-soluble and accumulates in fatty tissue. This affects how long it remains in your system but has a less clear relationship to the intensity of acute effects than many people believe.

**Metabolism** — Faster metabolism generally means faster onset and shorter duration for edibles, as the liver processes cannabinoids more quickly.

**Food intake** — Consuming edibles on a full stomach delays onset but can produce a more gradual, prolonged effect. An empty stomach speeds onset.

**Set and setting** — State of mind and environment significantly affect the cannabis experience, particularly at higher doses. Anxiety-prone environments or emotional states can amplify THC's anxiogenic potential.

**Genetics** — Variations in endocannabinoid receptor density and liver enzyme function mean some people are naturally more or less sensitive to THC.

